                "text": "I need to identify the enclosed regions in this 22x25 grid by mapping out where the boundary lines sit at each row and column, then flood-fill from the border to distinguish enclosed 0-cells (which become 2) from open ones (which become 3).\n\nTracing connectivity, I'm finding that rows 5-13 form a connected open region leaking through gaps at row9 and row12-13's missing column11, linking further into cols12-15 across rows10-13.\n\nChecking the boundaries, this whole area is fully enclosed\u2014col5 walls on the left, col16 on the right, row4 above, row14 below\u2014so this region gets filled with 2.\n\nThen for cols12-15 and cols17-21 in the same row band, both are similarly enclosed by walls on all four sides, so those also fill with 2.\n\nBut rows10-13 in cols17-21 is different: row13's gap at col22 lets it leak sideways, then row14's boundary doesn't fully cover that column range, so this area actually connects to the outside and should stay as 3, not get filled.\n\nThe upper region (rows1-3) also leaks out through gaps at row1 into the top opening, so it stays outside too.\n\nI'm now tracing whether the rows15-17 region near cols6-15 leaks out through gaps in row17 and row18's wall placement.\n\nEverything in that band cols6-15 rows15-16 connects out through those wall gaps, so it counts as outside, not enclosed.\n\nChecking the row9-13 cols6-15 block more carefully, the walls hold except for gaps at col11 rows12-13, which stay inside since they're bounded above and below by wall.\n\nSo the enclosed region is rows5-13, cols6-15 minus the wall cells themselves.\n\nNow I'm assembling the full grid row by row with these enclosed regions marked as 2 and walls as 7, everything else 3.",
